# threshold-unknown-label.tflw — CHECK-ONLY FIXTURE (M86): a `threshold … for "label"` clause whose
# label matches no `api` step in the same workload-bearing test — demonstrates `tflw check`'s
# teaching diagnostic (TF034: checker, `checkWorkloadTests`, tflw M43/D70).
#
# The typo is the point. A scoped threshold that matches nothing is not an error at runtime — it
# simply never binds to a step, so it can never breach, and the run goes green while the one
# latency budget anybody cared about was silently unenforced. Scoping is per-test: a label declared
# by another test's step does not satisfy this one.
#
# Run explicitly (dot-directory, invalid syntax — see bad-keyword.tflw's header for why):
#   npx tflw check tests/.checkonly/threshold-unknown-label.tflw

test "a threshold scoped to a step that isn't there"
  ramp to 1 users over 1s
  # Not decoration: as of tflw M89c a `duration` threshold — scoped or not — must be paired with
  # an unscoped `error rate` one (TF033). Without this line the fixture reports TF033 *and* TF034,
  # and stops isolating the one code it exists to demonstrate.
  threshold error rate is less than 1%
  threshold p95 duration for "checkotu" is less than 250ms
  api GET /health as "checkout"
  expect status equals 200
